// `->` reaches through a pointer to any depth. there is no `.` versus `->` distinction to
// remember: a struct value and a pointer to one are accessed the same way
// (book/concept/pointers_and_refs_v2.md, "Structs and classes")
//
// "any depth" was the part that did not hold. member lowering peeled exactly one level, so a
// `ptr<ptr<Point>>` base resolved to `ptr<Point>`, which is not a struct - the read failed with
// "Cannot access member 'x' of void type". the peeling is now a loop, and the type side asks
// AST::target_type_of rather than value_type_of, which is the same distinction: reading is one
// auto-deref, but `->` wants the struct however many addresses away it is

struct Point {
    int $x;
    int $y;
}

$pt = Point(1, 2);

ptr<Point> $p = &$pt;
ptr<ptr<Point>> $pp = &$p;
ptr<ptr<ptr<Point>>> $ppp = &$pp;

// every depth names the same field
echo $p->x;
echo $pp->x;
echo $ppp->x;

// and writing through any of them reaches the one Point
$p->x = 10;
echo $pt->x;

$pp->y = 20;
echo $pt->y;

$ppp->x = 30;
echo $pt->x;

// the pointer object itself has no members - `$p->x` is the pointee's field, never the
// pointer's. the address is what `:$` is for, and it is unaffected by any of the writes
echo ($p:$ == &$pt);
